      <h1 class="title">the pursuits of satiya+reed (ch.1)</h1>

      <p class="opener">Satiya shouts “Look!”</p>
      <p>And so Reed did just that as she froze mannequin-motionless on the sidewalk squinting at the subject of Satiya’s shoutout only a few steps before them.</p>
      <p><em>Unfortunate.</em> thought Reed.</p>
      <p><em>The atmospheric bubble I’d been carefully constructing has now popped, and the residue is too thinly-spread for immediate repair.</em></p>
      <p>Satiya’s shout interrupted Reed’s ramble which, depending on her audience, could have been taken for wonky or vivid. It was on the subject of her passion project, and her project is her passion. If she welcomes you to be a member of her audience as she paints the picture of her project’s process and progress, how could she allow herself to do so with a watered down delivery? Reed hates water paintings.</p>
      <p><em>They’re always so beautiful. But don’t you ever wonder how one might look without its vibrancy all drowned?</em></p>
      <p>Reed had woken up that day knowing for certain that she would lay out every detail to Satiya. After what happened the day before, Reed was feeling her sanguine-self drip just a bit.</p>
      <p><em>If this feeling sticks, I will find myself hanged in the MoMA!</em></p>
      <p>The performance was triggered when Satiya asked Reed to be her plus-one at her cousin’s funeral. “The main appetizer will be the service, and it will be followed by a train-ride entree served as we embark toward our destination in the desert for dessert.”</p>
      <p><em>What a strange sequence of events for a funeral.</em></p>
      <p>As though through telekineses, Satiya responded to Reed’s thought, “I don’t know when they became so celebratory, but we’ll be staying in an up-and-coming ghost town for the weekend. Everyone will have their own room, and I’ve never been to the desert before let alone spent a weekend with the ghosts…”</p>
      <p>Reed realized that she herself had also yet to see such a scene.</p>
      <p>“Would you like to go to the desert with me Reed?”</p>
      <p>Reed’s brain told Reed that she would in fact like to, but the thought lost itself on the way to her tongue.</p>
      <p>“My project is finally being birthed,” said Reed. “After six years of research, I can’t bear the thought of putting my brain in a space filled with the dead…”</p>
      <p>Satiya hmm’d.</p>
      <p>“Think of all the movies you’ve seen about possessed babies; I don’t want to preform an exorcism while I’m expecting.”</p>
      <p>Satiya mmm’d.</p>
      <p>“Although…” Reed in that moment realized it was indeed the perfect moment to flow into the pitch. “I suppose this final form is in a way nothing but a reincarnation of its former lives…” Reed stopped to think about the proposal again, but the thought was cut short by Satiya’s sixth sense:</p>
      <p>“No, that’s okay. A project is a priority…”</p>
      <p>Although Reed hadn’t formally begun its formal formation until only six months before that ramble on the sidewalk, she formulaically computed its conceptual logistics for five and a half years post-conception.</p>
      <p>“It’s brought me through quite the mental journey,” she told Satiya. “Some of those earlier forms really dragged me through parts of my brain I hadn’t known myself to be interested in; and yet, I’ve mind-mapped through those domains well-enough to consider myself an expert across three dozen fields.”</p>
      <p>This led Satiya to begin her own mind-mapping. Since Satiya had never been around for Reed’s research sessions, she had always been confused on how Reed fell into the tech-centric internships in the ways that Reed did. Even though Reed had always liked the humanities, she’d never shown interest in standing amongst the stems. Especially not after learning that painters see bushes, plants, and trees as being gateway drugs for watercolor works.</p>
      <p>About six months into her project, Reed called Satiya for some guidance. Of course, Reed didn’t discuss the project itself; instead, she spoke endlessly of the highs and lows of her new position with the LED manufacturing plant’s product engineering team.</p>
      <p>“Put it like this: my waist is the base and it cannot bear the weight. Sure, it’s exciting to work with my hands, but my feet can barely support such excitement.”</p>
      <p>Satiya hmm’d</p>
      <p>Surprised was Satiya because the fluorescent building was a seventy-minute train ride from Reed’s home. <em>What a dreadful commute.</em> Only a second after thinking about that cramped train pacing down a track buried half a mile below the ground, Surprised-Satiya fell into bed with Confused-Satiya. <em>When the fuck did Reed become an engineer?</em></p>
      <p>The time Reed spent building the prototype for a wireless electronic device taught her that if you were to hold a dense synthetic like memory foam for long enough, it would become quite warm. If you were to grip even harder, then maybe it would become so warm that it may act as the sole power source for some type of an electronic device’s internal energy system. Maybe if this ambiguous device were to be embedded within such a material, then the foam could act as a supplemental sensory element. By using this grip-able exterior to compliment the push buttons and haptic feedback sources that had already mapped out in the six variations of blueprints on Reed’s laptop, then the electronic device can function as both a universal remote, and a stress relieving, multi-sensorial toy.</p>
      <p><em>Have you ever been so frustrated by the poorly-mixed movies you watch in your home that you want to throw your remote across the room each time you’re forced to adjust the volume? What if you could simply take your anger out on the remote without disrupting your atmosphere? Boy do I have the thing for you…</em></p>
      <p>It clicked immediately, and she pushed the pitch for her million dollar business into her brain so that the idea couldn’t stray. Yet once she learned that such conductive powering could not be achieved, at least not with her bare resources, she figured that her project’s main objective of producing a product worthy of global dissemination would probably be best if applied to something more doable.</p>
      <p>“So while I was able to pull myself toward the engineers based on my research, I was unable to pull their bodies toward me without handing over a lump sum. How was I to afford to hire a thermal engineer? All those supplies ate up my funds.” She shrugged into the air she’d been walking into so hard that it added some resistance for several feet in front of her. This made her body drag beside Satiya’s, a motion that would prove handy momentarily.</p>
      <p>“So then I had to figure out what to do with the 24 sets of push-buttons sitting on the three queen-sized memory foam mattress toppers I found myself in possession of. Do you know how many come in a set?”</p>
      <p>Satiya, of course, had no idea. Her head shook only once before Reed matched the game of visual conversation initiated by Satiya and put every one of her ten fingers up. This meant that the energy drink Reed had been holding with her left hand was momentarily moved to Reed’s mouth where its lip was bitten down by Reed’s teeth. She didn’t close her lips over the can’s, of course; that would be inappropriate, even if it were to have only been a three second kiss.</p>
      <p>After those three seconds of no-contact, Reed gripped the can away from her mouth and back to the warmth of her hand in order to continue on dragging through her resistant breath that only got heavier as she sped through her speech. If the audience of her pitch were to become disengaged for even a second, then how would she bring them back into the atmosphere being constructed from her words?</p>
      <p>“While I tried fitting them into some of the ideas that followed, I fear that 240 buttons may be too far from relevancy for the project’s current and final form. How could you work memory foam and buttons into—”</p>
      <p>And then arrived Satiya’s shout. So the smog, thicker than ever, broke right as Reed was introducing the finale. Luckily, for Reed, the break came only after its assistance in softening Reed’s abrupt halt. Satiya had interrupted Reed’s story with only two feet of space between them and the thing on the ground being pointed at with the tip of Satiya’s middle finger. It was Satiya’s longest finger, and she always made sure to use that when directing someone’s attention towards something. While the thing being pointed at was still a blur, at least Satiya’s middle finger made very clear where that blur sat.</p>
      <p>It was a trick Satiya picked up when she was nineteen. In the six years since her birthday celebration, that middle finger became her best friend. Since she had just graduated high school, and had very adamantly rejected the notion of attending college, she spent a lot of time around people she’d always known. People who’d always expected her to be elsewhere because of her brainpower.</p>
      <p>Her parents, her younger sister, the worker in the corner store below her family’s apartment building, and three of her friends in the grade below her had all been vocal about their surprise to her adamancy for not pursuing higher-ed.</p>
      <p>“There’s just so much to be appreciated in the world that’s around us. Why should I spend all that time with nothing to observe other than backpacks and lecturers? No, I’m far too grateful for everything else.”</p>
      <p>While Reed saw no shame in switching course when necessary, Stubborn Satiya became tied to a lifelong pursuit bound in place only by her own adamancy.</p>
      <p>“I will become the most famed observer,” became Satiya’s own pitch. Ambitious Satiya looked to Thinking Satiya after she realized that such an ambitious ambition may require a strategy. This was especially necessary when Adi, the man at the corner store, told her that arriving at such a position could never happen.</p>
      <p>“If you are the one looking, then who would be looking at you? Who would you be famous to if all of it is for you?”</p>
      <p>Adi’s argument made Satiya squint in her thoughts and look around. Satiya had been convinced that her quick pitch would free herself from the interrogations of skeptics; but there she was, forced to improvise. And so her mind performed gymnastics in hopes of summersaulting into a rebuttal.</p>
      <p>However, perhaps as the result of telekinesis that Adi had also been keeping to himself, he gave a second consideration before she could stick a landing.</p>
      <p>“Besides,” he began after turning his attention from Satiya to the customer who’d just entered the store, “how would you get there without even being able to see yourself?”</p>
      <p>Satiya had also turned toward the customer in unison with Adi during his delivery. After thinking about what he’d said, Satiya decided to walk out of the store without turning back toward the counter. She couldn’t let Adi hear the thoughts in her head; they were too muddled with the realization that he was correct.</p>
      <p>She spent that night sitting in her room on her own memory foam mattress which was pressed against the apartment’s glass corner. It was always too hot at night for her to fall asleep comfortably, and it was that view from her room, facing the intersection above the corner store, that gave her all the observation-related training she’d needed since her family moved into the apartment on her ninth birthday.</p>
      <p>What would typically have been an ideal night for watching was instead blurred and smudged by Satiya’s salty eyes. The people she’d watched slowly distorted her vision so much that her own reflection became a smudge. Not only had her perception become wonky from Adi being right that night, but her body was more than ever bound to her apartment by the knots she herself tied. How could she unravel herself into the admittance of being wrong? She couldn’t.</p>
      <p>Catching sight of her empty and dissociated gaze out onto the street exactly where he had been standing, a man in some outfit of some color that couldn’t be made out stuck up a finger in Satiya’s direction. While he himself could not be any more smudged, that stuck up finger from the stuck up person was long enough for her to catch and throw right back at him.</p>
      <p>Just as clear as he was became everything in her line of sight. The world became a fog that only she could see.</p>
      <p>“A bird,” said Reed. “Isn’t it beautiful?”</p>
      <p>“One of the best I’ve ever seen,” replied Satiya.</p>
